A feature value is considered generalized if it includes more than one possible value in the original domain of the feature. For example, for the numerical feature ‘age’, if an original value could consist of the specific age 37 or 39, then the range 36–40 is a possible generalization for those values. It is less specific, as it includes five different values in the original domain. Similarly, if the categorical feature ‘marital status’ could originally contain any of the specific values ‘never married’, ‘married’ or ‘divorced’, a generalized value could consist of the group ‘never married OR divorced’, which includes two of the original values. This means that when divulging the generalized feature, the data subject gives less detailed information about their age or marital status.
There are essentially two types of generalizations, global recoding and local recoding. Global recoding means that the ranges for each feature are determined once for the entire dataset, i.e., a particular detailed value must be mapped to the same generalized value in all records. In this case we “force” the same generalizations for the entire domain, which may result in smaller ranges. Local recoding allows the same detailed value to be mapped to different generalized values in different groups. Having different ranges for different areas in the domain usually enables broader generalizations.
2.2  Information loss metrics
Many metrics for measuring information loss have been developed. A recent 2018 survey [39] noted 78 distinct metrics, while grouping them into different categories and intended uses. The Classification Metric (CM) [21] is suitable when the purpose of the generalized data is to train a classifier. Each record is assigned a class label, and information loss is computed based on the adherence of a tuple to the majority class of its group. The Discernibility Metric (DM) [5] measures the cardinality of the equivalence class.
