We measured this risk for three datasets: the two generalized datasets corresponding to the results presented in Table 3, and the original GSS dataset. For the original GSS dataset, with no generalizations applied, there were 2085 distinct records out of 2446 total records in the validation set, corresponding to a risk score of 0.788. The generalized dataset corresponding to no accuracy loss contains 2054 distinct records and has a risk score of 0.771; only slightly lower than the original data. However, when looking at the dataset that was generated with 2% accuracy loss, the result is much more remarkable. It contains only 222 disctinct records and has a risk score of 0.047; a very significant reduction, of more than an order of magnitude, in the disclosure risk. This demonstrates that our method has value in protecting individuals’ privacy in addition to helping companies comply with regulations.

4.4  Personalized data minimization

In this section we introduce an additional approach to apply generalizations on newly collected data, namely dynamic generalization. Once the generalized feature set has been determined by the basic process (described in Sect. 3 and data is being collected from a certain user, the actual generalized value of a certain feature can be used to dynamically improve the generalizations for the remaining features. This is possible because a feature’s generalizations may have been restricted by domains that are no longer relevant.
Let’s look at an example, presented in Fig. 5. In this example there are two features, age and gender, and four possible labels colored red, yellow, green, and blue. The generalizations produced by the non-dynamic procedure are depicted by the black lines. Without any prior information, the data subject would need to reveal his/her age in a fiveyear range and exact gender. However, if gender is collected first, the generalizations for age are improved from four to

Fig. 5  Example data for dynamic generalization
three ranges, and, in some cases, only a 10-years range is required. For example, a female user may reveal her age in the range of 20–30 years old. Alternatively, if we ask for age first, in some cases the gender may not be needed at all. For example, a 35–40 year old person would not be asked to reveal their gender, since it is not needed to determine their class (in this case blue).
This approach can be carried out while collecting new data from a data subject, for example using a dynamic form. In the beginning, the user is presented with a form containing all required features alongside the proposed ranges or subsets of values for each. Each time the user chooses the appropriate generalized value for a feature, a process is triggered to calculate new, improved generalizations for the remaining features. Generalizations presented to the user are thus dynamically updated based on the user’s input for previous features.
The order of filling in the feature values may be determined by the user. By first revealing the values of features the user finds less sensitive, the more sensitive features gradually become more generalized or even not required at all. In this manner, we achieve a truly personalized data minimization, allowing different users to reveal more or less about different aspects of their lives based on their own personal preference. Another alternative is to allow the organization collecting the data to decide the order of the features, for example based on cost.
Note that in some cases, when applying dynamic generalization, it may be beneficial to avoid (or limit) regular feature selection before applying the generalization process. Feature selection that does not take into account the user’s (or organization’s) preferences regarding the choice and order of features may end up throwing out features that are not especially sensitive or problematic to collect, leaving the more sensitive or problematic features. It may therefore be preferable to let the user or organization choose from all available features, thus allowing them maximum flexibility.
