5.4  Privacy‑guided building of decision trees
In our implementation, we trained a regular decision tree, then applied privacy-related metrics (such as information loss) to the resulting tree to find good generalizations of the data. Clearly, the more splits a tree contains for a certain feature, the greater number of ranges it will have in the resulting generalization. Using the features’ sensitivity measure as additional input when constructing the tree, we might have ended up with a better generalization for the sensitive features. Replacing the Gini index or Information Gain metric used when deciding what split to create with a metric that takes into consideration the sensitivity level of features, such as the method described in [17], could result in such an improvement.
5.5  Minimization of training data
The approach outlined in this paper does not change the training data or the trained model, rather it only affects new data collected for analysis.
If the target model is retrained some time after the generalization process was performed, it is recommended to retrain the generalizer as well and derive new generalizations that are best suited for the new model. It is of course possible to continue using the same generalizations, however the model’s accuracy may be somewhat affected.
If the ML model needs to be retrained from time to time on fresh user data, it is also possible to use the collected and generalized data to retrain the model. In theory, since the generalizations were chosen such that the accuracy of the model is not degraded, using it for training should also not degrade the model’s accuracy too much, unless there is a significant drift in the data’s distribution. We leave validation of this approach to future work. If a significant data drift is however probable, we recommend starting the process from scratch, i.e., collecting the complete data for training a new model, and deriving new generalizations from that model.
6  Related work
A great body of prior work has focused on protecting the privacy of training sets used to train ML models. Several attacks were shown to be able to reveal either whether an individual was part of the training set (membership attacks) or infer certain possibly sensitive properties of the training data (attribute inference attacks) [14, 13, 32, 33]. Therefore, many methods have been proposed to anonymize or de-identify the training data, including tailored anonymization of training data [21, 26], adding noise or applying differential privacy to the training process [43, 1, 29], and many more. However, all of these techniques are aimed at protecting the

training set and do not provide any protections for runtime data, i.e., newly collected data to which the model is applied.
Another focus area has been performing training and/ or prediction on encrypted data [8, 44, 4, 19]. These approaches do not solve the issue of collecting less data to begin with. They simply offer better protection for the data that is already collected and stored.
Other works have focused on feature selection and dimensionality reduction, also called feature extraction [27]. These are aimed at fulfilling the principle of parsimony by reducing the number of features used as input to the model. However, they do so mainly to improve the performance and memory footprint of the model, not with privacy in mind. These methods sometimes produce a reduced set of features, but they cannot reduce the granularity of the collected data. Embedding is another method to achieve dimensionality reduction for high-dimensional data, the most famous being LLE [31] and word2vec [28].
Feature abstraction [2, 35] can also be used for the purpose of dimensionality reduction. These methods sometimes result in different abstraction levels of the original features. Until now, such techniques have been employed with the goal of improving model accuracy and not privacy. Feature discretization [12, 24] is concerned with the discretization of continuous features.
More recently, the domain of knowledge distillation [18] has been established, that includes a set of techniques for compressing large, complex models to more compact, less resource-intensive models, that can run for example on devices with limited resources. Many of those techniques are based on teacher-student architectures, where a small student model learns from a larger teacher model.
Techniques for increasing explainability of models by extracting comprehensible, symbolic representations from trained neural networks are also often based on inducing a decision tree that approximates the concept represented by the network [9]. Similarly, assessing the interpretability of ML models [34] may also involve creating explanatory (surrogate) models that are more interpretable than the target model and then measuring the fidelity between the explanatory model and the predictive model. Typically, decision trees are used as surrogate models in this context.
Pratesi et al. [30] proposed a system enabling a privacyaware ecosystem for sharing personal data. It is based on assessing both the empirical privacy risk associated with users represented in the data, and the data quality guaranteed only with users not at risk. They enable the data provider to select a transformation that yields an adequate tradeoff between data quality and privacy risk. Bakker et al. [3] propose a framework for limiting the amount of data collected in a data acquisition scenario, by redistributing the budget across individuals in a way that ensures fairness of the resulting model.

