Finally, the UK information commissioner’s office’s guidance on the AI auditing framework from 2021 [22] finds that the data minimization principle should be applied both at the training and inference stages, and suggests converting personal data into less ‘human readable’ formats, making inferences locally and privacy-preserving query approaches as possible solutions for inference time data minimization. However, none of these approaches consider that less information may be collected to begin with, while still enabling the model to make predictions with the same (or very close) level of accuracy.
7  Conclusion and future work
In this work we presented a novel method to perform data minimization for ML models in a manner that minimizes the effect on model accuracy. Our approach is complementary to standard feature selection and extraction techniques: in addition to suppressing certain features, it can lead to the generalization of other features, thus further reducing the amount and granularity of collected data. This both helps organizations to adhere to the data minimization principle, as well as reduce data collection, storage, and management costs.
Data minimization also helps to prevent unfair data collection practices such as excessive collection or retention of data, as well as reduce the personal risk to data subjects in case of a data breach. Generalizing the input data to models could also potentially help prevent prediction bias or other forms of discrimination, leading to more fairness-aware or discrimination-aware data mining practices.
Our method is generic and can be applied to any dataset and any ML model, although the experiments shown in this paper cover only classifiers. We have shown that our method was able to achieve good results—finding data to minimize without impacting accuracy—on different types of classification models. Evaluation on other types of models (e.g., regression, generation, etc.) is left for future work.
The main limitation of this work is that there is no guarantee that any generalization can be performed without harming the model’s accuracy. There may indeed be cases where no possible generalizations are found. However, it is still important for compliance purposes to be able to demonstrate that such an analysis has been performed.
Another shortcoming could be the simplicity of the surrogate model used, i.e., univariate decision trees. This may be too simplistic a model to be able to accurately depict the complex trends learned by a deep neural network. To solve this issue we propose to investigate the use of additional types of surrogate models, as discussed in Sect. 5.2.
This is a very initial implementation of data minimization for ML, leaving many areas of possible improvement. These

include for example further optimization of the tree pruning and feature removal processes (described in Sect. 5.3) as well as using the features’ sensitivity measure as additional input when constructing the generalization model (detailed in Sect. 5.4).
Additional areas for future research may also include looking at other techniques from the domains of knowledge distillation and model explainability and interpretability for the purpose of data minimization. Another direction could be to look at techniques for generating adversarial examples as an alternative way to discover the decision boundaries of a model. This could be useful, for example, for extending this research into non-tabular data. Finally, data minimization for ML model training data may also be an interesting avenue of research.
